; %FINDCORE returns the frame's number as a fixnum (sys/ucadr/uc-page-fault.lisp,
; XFINDCORE, which returns FINDCORE's M-B as COREFOUND2 pushed it, with
; DTP-FIX), and PAGE-IN-WORDS pages a run of pages in with it.  Load
; lisp/findcore-fixnum.lisp first.  QUUX's line found its FINDCORE returning
; a word of data type 0, which PAGE-IN-WORDS' arithmetic took to ILLOP; the
; CADR's returns a fixnum before and after its scan pointer wraps (measured
; on System 1003), and this check keeps it so: with a %FINDCORE that returns
; data type 0, System 1003's band never reaches its TELNET prompt, so the
; check fails before its cases (measured).  PAGE-IN-WORDS is reached
; whenever its range holds a page not in core, as most of P-N-STRING's pages
; are after a boot; the window system and the GC page areas and arrays in
; through it.
;
; The control: four pages of P-N-STRING in a row are not in core.
(not (null (findcore-out-run 4))) => T
(findcore-type) => T
; The four paged in by PAGE-IN-WORDS in one transfer, each equal to its
; blocks in the PAGE partition.
(findcore-page-in 4) => (T T)
